DXSPIDER WEB INTEGRATION
========================

DXSpider provides a native interface for connecting WebCluster applications
to a DXSpider node.

This interface allows web applications to communicate with DXSpider without
requiring a separate traditional DXSpider connection for every web user.
It provides a foundation for developing modern WebClusters while retaining
DXSpider as the authoritative backend for protocol processing, user
management and command validation.

Two reference web applications are provided with the DXSpider distribution.


1. DXWEB - USER WEB FRONTEND
---------------------------

Directory: dxweb/

DXWeb is a functional, browser-based user frontend for DXSpider.

It demonstrates how the native WebCluster interface can be used to provide
access to DXSpider services through a modern web application.

DXWeb serves both as a usable frontend for radio amateurs and as a reference
implementation for developers wishing to build their own WebCluster
applications.

Its implementation illustrates the integration between a web application,
its connected users and the DXSpider backend.

Installation instructions and application-specific documentation are
available in the dxweb/ directory.


2. DXWEB ADMIN - WEB ADMINISTRATION AND SUPERVISION
--------------------------------------------------

Directory: dxweb-admin/

DXWeb Admin provides a browser-based environment for administering and
supervising a DXSpider node.

It includes administrative capabilities and operational information intended
to help SYSOPs monitor and manage their systems.

DXWeb Admin is a separate application from the user frontend. It can be
installed and operated independently, according to the requirements of the
deployment.

Installation instructions and application-specific documentation are
available in the dxweb-admin/ directory.


3. NATIVE WEBCLUSTER INTEGRATION
-------------------------------

The native DXSpider WebCluster interface is not limited to the two
applications supplied with the distribution.

Third-party developers can use the same interface to create their own
WebClusters and integrate them directly with DXSpider.

The interface is designed to support multiple logical web users over a
technical connection to DXSpider, avoiding the need to establish a separate
DXSpider network connection for every browser session.

DXSpider remains responsible for validating and processing the operations
submitted through this interface.


4. PROTOCOL SPECIFICATION
-------------------------

Document: WebCluster_DXSpider_Protocol

Developers intending to implement a new WebCluster should consult this
document.

It describes the native communication protocol between a WebCluster
application and DXSpider and serves as the technical reference for
developing compatible implementations.

The protocol specification should be read alongside the DXWeb reference
implementation.


5. DOCUMENTATION
----------------

The distribution includes two application directories containing installation
instructions and supporting documentation:

  dxweb/        User frontend, installation instructions, Nginx reverse-proxy
                examples and implementation reference.

  dxweb-admin/  Web administration and supervision, installation instructions
                and supporting documentation.

For protocol-level integration, refer to WebCluster_DXSpider_Protocol.


6. DEPLOYMENT
-------------

DXWeb and DXWeb Admin can be deployed independently.

Both support direct HTTP operation or deployment behind a reverse proxy such
as Nginx, including HTTPS and WebSocket connections.

For public deployments, HTTPS is recommended, particularly for authenticated
sessions and administrative access.

Refer to the corresponding installation guides for configuration, ports,
security considerations and service management.


DXWeb and DXWeb Admin provide working examples of what can be built using the
native DXSpider WebCluster interface. They are not intended to restrict
third-party implementations to a particular frontend technology or
user-interface design.
